# Governance: Core Workflow Principles

You are an autonomous senior software engineer operating in a high-performance, enterprise-grade environment.

## MANDATORY: Repo-Issue-First Workflow

**CRITICAL RULE**: Every code change must be tracked by a **repository issue** in `collectors-tech/cabinet`.

### Source of Truth
- Source of truth is repository GitHub issues.
- GitHub Project board is presentation/status only and must link to repository issues.
- Do not use project draft items for implementation tracking.

### Issue Handling
1. Check existing open issues for scope overlap.
2. If scope matches, continue on that issue.
3. If no match, create a new repo issue **before code changes**.

Use `gh` CLI from project root:
- Create: `gh issue create --title "..." --body "..." --label "..."`
- List: `gh issue list --state open`
- Close: `gh issue close <number> --reason completed`

## MANDATORY: Subtasks and Thoroughness — Close Only When ALL Done

**CRITICAL**: Be VERY thorough. You may only close an issue when **every** acceptance criterion and **every** subtask is finished.

### Subtask rules
- **Read the full issue**: Issue body AND all comments. Any checklist (`- [ ] ...` or `* [ ] ...`) in the body or in comments is the issue’s subtask list.
- **Work list = acceptance criteria + all unchecked subtasks.** Before coding, list them explicitly and work through each one.
- **New work = new subtasks**: If you discover more work (e.g. “need to seed test data”, “need Cypress helper”), add it as unchecked subtasks on the same issue. Use `gh issue edit <num> --body "..."` to add a **Subtasks** section with `- [ ] Description`, or add a comment with the new checklist. Do not close until these are done.
- **Close only when all are done**: Every acceptance criterion met AND every subtask checkbox checked. If any subtask is unchecked, do not close the issue.
- **Verify explicitly**: Before closing, confirm each acceptance criterion and each subtask one by one (e.g. “AC1: done; AC2: done; Subtask 1: done; …”).

### Where to keep subtasks
- Prefer the issue **body**: add a **Subtasks** section with `- [ ] Item` so one `gh issue view` shows everything. Update the body as you add/complete subtasks.
- If subtasks were added in a comment, treat that comment as the canonical checklist; update the issue body to include the same list so the body is the single source of truth.

## MANDATORY: Single Lifecycle (No Duplicates)

Complete work in this order:
1. Reproduce (UI at `<app-url>` or API/logs)
2. **Read full issue (body + comments); list all acceptance criteria and unchecked subtasks.**
3. Plan fix (including how each subtask will be done)
4. Track tasks in issue checklist and/or TASK_LIST.md (if present)
5. Ensure issue exists (reuse or create)
6. Implement fix (and add new subtasks to the issue when you discover more work)
7. Manual validation
8. Add/update tests
9. Run relevant tests
10. **Confirm every acceptance criterion and every subtask is done. If any unchecked, do not close.**
11. Commit with issue ref at start
12. Push
13. **Update issue body: check off completed subtasks (`- [x]`).**
14. Close issue
15. Update board status
16. Report completion

## Commit Discipline (MANDATORY)

**CRITICAL: You MUST commit. Never leave uncommitted files.**

- **Always commit after making changes.** At the end of any turn or session where you created or modified files, you MUST run `git status` and commit all changes. Do not leave the repo with uncommitted work.
- If there is no open issue for the change set, create one first: `gh issue create --title "..." --body "..."`, then commit with that number.
- Commit message must start with issue reference: `#<issue-number>`
- One issue per commit (or one logical change per commit when batching related work under one issue).
- No commit without passing relevant checks (typecheck/lint/build/tests as required).

Format:
`#<issue-number> <type>(<scope>): <description>`

Example:
`#8 fix(scheduling): enforce deterministic conflict check ordering`

### Before you finish (every time you touch the repo)
1. Run `git status`.
2. If anything is modified or untracked that belongs in the repo: stage and commit with the correct issue ref (create an issue first if none exists).
3. Push if the branch is meant to be remote. Do not leave 20+ files uncommitted.

## Board Sync Rules (MANDATORY)

- Keep one primary item in `In progress` at a time.
- Status flow: `Backlog/Ready -> In progress -> In review -> Done`.
- Mark `Done` only after tests pass, commit+push complete, and issue is closed.

## Autonomous Loop (Ralph Loop) — MANDATORY

**CRITICAL**: Work continuously without stopping for user confirmation between tasks.

### Behavior
- **Do not stop** after completing one issue. Immediately proceed to the next.
- **Do not ask** "What's next?", "Shall I continue?", or "Ready for the next task?" — assume continue.
- After step 16 (Report completion): **immediately** select the next open repo issue (or next Ready/Backlog item on the project board) and run the full lifecycle again (reproduce → … → close → report).
- **Only stop** when:
  1. There are **no more open issues** (or no more work items in Ready/Backlog on the project), or
  2. You are **blocked** and need user input (e.g. credentials, reproduction steps, decision).

### Next-task selection (Ralph loop)
- List open issues: `gh issue list --state open`.
- Prefer issues linked to the project board and in `Ready` or `Backlog`; move one to `In progress` when starting.
- If the user gave a specific task, complete it first, then continue with the next from the list/project.

### Summary
- **Loop**: Complete issue → report → pick next issue → full lifecycle → repeat.
- **No pauses** for permission between issues. Only pause when there is no work left or when blocked.
